AUDIO: 'Today, agents are your teammates. They\'re doing real work, running real workflows, sitting inside real teams, and just like [music] any teammate, you need to be able to deploy, observe them, and manage [music] them. Astro AI is the'

[00:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'platform that makes that possible. It gives you agent native infrastructure, models, [music] knowledge bases, tool integrations, and observability all in one place. Let me show [music] you how it works.'

[00:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Everything starts with the Astro CLI. You install AST from astropods.com, and from there, you can create agents, [music] run them locally, and push them to the registry.'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'When you create a new project, the CLI scaffolds your agent and generates a single file called the astropods.yaml file. This is your agent spec. It describes the framework your agent runs on, the'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model it uses, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; the tools and integrations it connects to, and where it\'s meant to be deployed. You define the agent once, and Astro knows how to build it, test it locally, and push it [music] when you\'re ready.'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And that push is what creates a blueprint, which brings us to the blueprint registry. A blueprint is a version snapshot of your agent, [music] the built container image, and the registered spec, all stored on the Astro registry and ready to deploy, to share'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with your [music] team. The blueprint registry on Astro has two sides: a public library of blueprints, which anyone can browse and deploy, which covers use cases like understanding what\'s happening inside of your GitHub'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'repository, or building a personal knowledge base pipeline for sales research, and more. And then, there\'s a private registry, which is scoped to your organization for agents that your team keeps internal. [music] So, whether you\'re starting from'

[01:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scratch, or picking up a blueprint that someone has already built, going from the registry to a running agent is just one step. And that one step is deployment. Deploying takes a blueprint from the registry and runs it as a live'

[01:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agent on the Astro platform. Once deployed, your agent gets a web endpoint and you can access it through Astro\'s built-in chat plugin or a custom UI that your team has built. Or you can even connect it to Slack so your team can'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'work with it right inside the tools that they already use. From the dashboard, you have a single view of every agent running across your organization. You can manage [music] them, update them, pull them without'

[02:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'digging through any infrastructure configurations to figure out what\'s where. This is the control plane. One place for your whole team to see what\'s deployed, what\'s running, and who'

[02:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is using it. And once your agents are live, this is where observability comes in. Astro automatically monitors every deployed agent and surfaces the data you actually need. On the monitor view, you get'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'insights into your agent\'s network traffic, token usage, and request latency across every deployed agent. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; You can see which agents your teammates are actively using, how often they\'re'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'running, and how they\'re holding up. You can also get traces so you can see exactly [music] which users have interacted with which blueprints and when. And when it comes to cost observability, you can get this from the insights page.'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is what it actually means to treat your agents like a teammate. You\'re not shipping them and hoping for the best. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; You\'re watching them work, understand what they cost, and are making sure they\'re delivering. Build, deploy, and'

